Hi Lochan,

> I am curious if we can use the patient-reported sleep log aka guider (bedtime, wakeuptime) to calculate the sleep efficiency manually without providing it to GGIR.
>
> The main GGIR vignette provides the following formula for calculating the sleep efficiency.
>
> Sleep Efficiency = SleepDurationInSpt / guider_inbedDuration
>
> where,
> SleepDurationInSpt = derived from actigraphy
> guider_inbedDuration = derived from sleep log (guider_inbedEnd - guider_inbedStart i.e. time period between bedtime and wakeup time in sleep log)
> Can we use the same formula to calculate sleep efficiency of study participants manually?

What do you mean by manually? Manually based on sleep diary is not possible because you need sleep during SPT, manually based on GGIR output requires that GGIR had access to a sleeplog with time in bed?

> I also have one more doubt, does GGIR compute a different SleepDurationInSpt when we use the guider or sleep log?

If the sleeplog is available then that is the guider. Next, GGIR looks for overlap with the 'sustained inactivity bouts' (sib) after which the start of the first sib and the end of the last sib defined the SPT window. If a sleeplog is used with time in bed then that allows for calculating sleep efficiency.

We tried to document this in the vignette: https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GGIR/vignettes/GGIR.html#56_Sleep_analysis
If it is not clear, then you could either help us to understand which parts of the documentation are not clear. Further, you may want to consider signing up to our GGIR training course where we discuss this: https://www.accelting.com/ggir-training/

With kind regards,

Vincent

